A||||12||falconhulk||Help!!  Stalling fiero||||1||
Z||000000||falconhulk||08-13-2001||08:55 AM||||I just bought a 85 se Fiero over the weekend.  It only has 53,000 miles and looks perfect.  It ran fine for the 100 miles home.<BR>It seemed to use a lot of gas.  I drove it for about 15 miles the day after I came home and it was also fine.  Today on the way to work it stalled at stoplights 4 times.  The check engine light came on and went off maybe 8 times.  The was a strange smell from the engine that I cannot easily identify.  Please help!!  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||JohnF||08-13-2001||09:00 AM||||Your cat is probably kaput!  Nice find, though.||||reg||14
Z||000002||falconhulk||08-13-2001||09:02 AM||||Forgot to add that it has all new plugs and wires.  It has good accelaration also.  Just for FYI it has been raining here like crazy.<BR>Not sure if this could be related.....maybe something got wet?  After stalling it starts right back up.  There is a strange smell that I cannot place also.||||reg||1
Z||000003||falconhulk||08-13-2001||09:03 AM||||I thought it was the CAT!!!!  I was going to replace it with flexable pipe yesterday but it rained.  Just my luck||||reg||1
Z||000004||JohnF||08-13-2001||02:02 PM||||Water in the fuel is easy to spot.  Just use a flexible hose to suck a little gas from the bottom of the tank into a clear quart jar.  Water will jump out at you almost.  Rubbing alchohol gets rid of it slowly.<P>Still bet on the cat!||||reg||8
Z||000005||falconhulk||08-14-2001||03:19 PM||||Ok I went drove home last night with the only problem being a noticeable loss of power.  I plan to hollow out the cat or replace it with flex pipe.  I am also going to replace the fuel and air filter just to be safe. My wife called the pontiac dealer and he said it does not sound like the cat!  To me it sounds like a classic cat problem:<P>1.  The smell<BR>2.  The stalling<BR>3.  The loss of power<P>What else could casue this?  Not sure if he was just messing with my wife because she is a woman.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fformula88||08-14-2001||04:29 PM||||Sounds like it sure could be a cat converter.  Since the engine light was on, the computer will have a code stored.  Its really easy to pull the code yourself, and if you don't have a repair manual we can tell you how to pull it.<P>Is your SE a V6?  I am thinking this could also possible be a vacumn problem, like the EGR tube or a plastic line cracked.||||reg||1
Z||000007||falconhulk||08-15-2001||09:47 AM||||Yep sure is a V6.  Problem with the check engine light is it was kind of flickering and only stayed solid for like 1 minute.  The stalling took place after this and the light was not on when this happened.  Also I dont have the repair manual yet but have ordered.  Where is the fuelfilter?  I tried to trace it back from the injectors but did not see it.<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||08-15-2001||11:12 AM||||Okay, here's a shot in the dark...<P>If it's an automatic, it could be the torque converter clutch (TCC) solenoid.<BR>When you stop, does it shudder to a halt?<BR>If it only has 53,000 miles, it hasn't been driven much. TCC solenoids like to hang when they aren't driven. (At least mine did.)<BR>Only about $40 for the part, and I understand it's a couple of hours to replace it. <BR>The quick and dirty fix is to unplug the connector from the tranny. (Square, 4-pin connector on the very front, towards the firewall.) You can drive it that way, temporarily, but your gas mileage will suffer. <P>This only addresses the stalling problem, not the other symptoms.  <P><BR>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fformula88||08-15-2001||05:06 PM||||As long as the CEL stayed on and the engine hadn't stalled yet, it should have stored a code.<P>Pull the black trim piece off from around the cigarette lighter.  Take a paperclip and jump the two lower terminals on the passenger side of the plug thats in there.  Then turn the key to the on position but don't start the car.  The CEL light will start flashing a sequence of numbers.  It will flash the firast digit, then the second and have a pause.  It flashes each number three times consecutively.  The first is 12... 1 flash a pause and two flashes followed by a long pause.  After it flashes 12 three times, it will flash any stored codes.  Numbers range from 12 which means it has no codes to 55.  If you read them and post I'll tell you what the code is.  It could be a good insight!<P>Fuel filter is loceted underneath the car in front of the passenger side rear wheel along the bottom of the firewall.||||reg||1
Z||000010||84Bill||08-15-2001||06:44 PM||||Add power and decrease pitch angle  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000011||falconhulk||08-17-2001||09:34 AM||||Ok I replaced the airfilter and it has not stalled again.  Still have not been able to look at the cat.  What should me oil pressure guage read normally?  Before it was fluctuating slightly between 45 and 70.  No its steady at 80.  Is this to high?   I am not sure what the standard pressure is for a fiero.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||85 gt fiero||08-17-2001||08:53 PM||||yes that is kind of high, its supposed to be around 55-60psi not 80, have you checked the exhaust manifolds for any leaks or broken bolts i had that happen to mine and the oil pressure went up it stalled and it hesitated when you gave it gas.       Hope you get it fixed.||||reg||1
